[node name="CafeGatheringEncounterStage" instance=ExtResource("1_stage")]
editor_description = "Encounter 9, depth 14,000: the Cheese Cafe. The Miner breaks through into Das Queso Cafe and finds everybody he met on the way down already there - Cheese Girl serving from the window, the Treasure Hunter with nothing left to carry, Rotini arriving with his colony, Quibble, and the Lantern Keeper standing apart at the open end of the room.

THE SHOT IS A PAN, AND THE LAYOUT IS THE PAN. Zephan's IMG_1647 plans this beat as a camera move across the gathering that ends on the Lantern Keeper, who delivers his line and 'goes shoom'. The encounter camera cannot pan - it centres on the miner's landing column and holds - so the move is authored into the LAYOUT instead. The cast are laid out left to right in the exact order they speak, so reading the conversation walks the eye across the room and finishes on the Keeper at the far right, who then fades where he stands.

Left to right: the storefront with Cheese Girl in its window, the Treasure Hunter, Rotini, the MINER, Quibble, the Lantern Keeper, and the cafe's outdoor table. That is also the order depth_encounter_config lists Gathering Actor Ids in, which is not a coincidence: the schedule's roster order and this room's left-to-right order are the same fact, and integration_verify holds them to it. Move somebody here and the roster moves with them.

SPACING IS MEASURED, NOT EYEBALLED. Every gap is one miner-width of daylight, the house rule from the authoring guide: root to root is (a + b) / 2 + 52.8 against the measured bodies - miner 52.8, Rotini 62.7, Treasure Hunter 86.5, Quibble 61.5, Keeper 100.4. Run local_tests/measure_actor_heights.gd before moving anybody, because those numbers move when art does.

TRACKING IS ON, AND THIS ROOM NEEDS IT MORE THAN ANY OTHER. The snaking fall arrives down any column in a 49-cell band and the camera centres on it, so a gathering pinned to the room sits up to 192px off centre every run - and with five bodies at a fixed pitch the miner regularly lands standing inside one of them. Conversation Tracks Miner slides ActorMarkers to his column and Props Track Tracked Cast carries the storefront and the furniture under the same shift, so the whole cafe holds its shape around him at every landing.

CHEESE GIRL IS DRAWN INTO THE SET. She is painted into the storefront's serving window, so Actors Drawn Into Set names her: the cutscene editor skips her stand-in, and DepthEncounterController places her mark but leaves her presenter hidden. Her line still plays and the box still attributes it to her. Delete the DasQuesoCafe node and she has to come out of that list in the same change, or she disappears from the scene altogether.

THE RATS ARE NOT AUTHORED HERE. Rotini's persistent colony has been following the player since 11,200 and is standing around the miner through this whole shot, which is what fills the room and dresses the table. Nothing in this scene places one.

Reference: Zephan's IMG_1647 (the cafe boards, including the storefront and the table of mice) and IMG_1646 (the crowd)."
opening_move_seconds = 0.4
closing_move_seconds = 1.2
opening_step_height = 5.0
conversation_pose = &"idle"
hide_actor_after_closing = true
closing_fade_seconds = 1.2
entrance_facing = -1
conversation_facing = -1
closing_facing = -1
conversation_tracks_miner = true
conversation_root_offset_from_miner_x = 244.0
props_track_tracked_cast = true
sequence = ExtResource("12_sequence")
actors_drawn_into_set = Array[StringName]([&"cheese_girl"])

[node name="Entrance" parent="ActorMarkers" index="0"]
editor_description = "The same mark as Conversation. Canonical story says the Keeper HAS gathered here, so he is already standing at the end of the room when the ceiling comes in - there is no approach to walk, and Opening Move Seconds only exists to keep a tween honest if the timeline is ever cleared."
position = Vector2(68, 0)

[node name="Conversation" parent="ActorMarkers" index="1"]
editor_description = "The Lantern Keeper's stand, 244px right of the miner - one measured miner-width off Quibble, who is himself one off the miner. The authored x is where he lands for a dead-centre fall: the stage sits 22 cells right of the terrain centre, so the miner is at -176 here. Conversation Tracks Miner re-derives the whole root's shift from wherever this marker currently is, so the number is for reading the scene rather than for the result."
position = Vector2(68, 0)

[node name="Work" parent="ActorMarkers" index="2"]
editor_description = "The cafe table. Kept as the mark for any later beat that walks somebody over to sit down."
position = Vector2(224, 0)

[node name="Rest" parent="ActorMarkers" index="3"]
position = Vector2(68, 0)

[node name="Exit" parent="ActorMarkers" index="4"]
editor_description = "His own mark. He does not leave by walking - Closing Fade Seconds hops him once and fades him out where he stands, which is Zephan's 'and then he goes shoom' and the same departure his warning at 5,600 uses."
position = Vector2(68, 0)

[node name="cheese_girl" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="5"]
editor_description = "The serving window, 20px right of the storefront's centre, where she is painted. Her presenter is placed here and left HIDDEN, because Actors Drawn Into Set names her. The mark exists so the schedule has somewhere to say she is and so the roster's left-to-right order stays true. Named for her actor id, which is how DepthEncounterController finds it and how an ordinary text search for the character finds her mark along with everything else about her."
position = Vector2(-589, 0)

[node name="treasure_hunter" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="6"]
editor_description = "Between the shop and Rotini, 127px off the rat: (86.5 + 62.7) / 2 + 52.8. He stands nearest the cafe because he is the one who has stopped - 'No treasure here. Good. I was tired of carrying things.'"
position = Vector2(-413, 0)

[node name="rutini" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="7"]
editor_description = "On the miner's left, 110px off him: (52.8 + 62.7) / 2 + 52.8 measures 110.5. He arrived WITH the player - his colony has been digging alongside since 11,200 - so he is the only one here who is not waiting at the cafe but coming into it, and 'Easy, lads. We're here' is him calling them to a stop."
position = Vector2(-286, 0)

[node name="coffee_cat" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="8"]
editor_description = "The miner's right, 110px off him: (52.8 + 61.5) / 2 + 52.8. Quibble measures 61.5px wide now; the authoring guide's spacing table still lists the old 202px, which was corrected on the encounter 7 branch. Re-measure before trusting either number."
position = Vector2(-66, 0)

[node name="DasQuesoCafe" parent="PropMarkers" index="0" instance=ExtResource("5_cafe")]
editor_description = "The shop the whole run has been walking toward, and the one thing in this scene that must never be cropped, because its sign is the payoff. Its right edge stands one miner-width clear of the Treasure Hunter and its left edge lands 68px inside the frame at a dead-centre landing.

SCALE AND THE ROOM ARE ONE DECISION. 0.1232 makes the 1626x1558 art 200px wide and 192px tall - twenty four cells, and two thirds again the miner's own 116px, so it reads as a building rather than as a hut. The room was cut to hold it: local_tests/carve_cafe_gathering_room.gd lifts the ceiling to twenty six cells over every column this building can slide to and prints the worst one. Changing this scale means re-running that script, not nudging this number, and the script fails rather than saves if the two ever disagree.

Y IS ZERO AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, unlike the bench in encounter 5. A prop is not floor-sampled, so one authored on the dig line normally sits knee-deep in the loose rock the cast stand on. This is a BUILDING, and a building bedded into the ground reads better than one perched on top of it. The cast, who are sampled, stand about 23px higher than its foot, which is what puts them in front of its awning rather than level with its roof.

Z 2, one behind the cast, because it is a backdrop they stand in front of rather than something anybody is occluded by. Zephan's directive to move every character onto the second stratum will change what the cast's own order is; when that lands, re-check this against them rather than against the number."
z_index = 2
position = Vector2(-609, 0)
scale = Vector2(0.1232, 0.1232)

[node name="CafeTableShop" parent="PropMarkers" index="1" instance=ExtResource("3_table")]
z_index = 0
editor_description = "SECOND STRATUM. Zephan asked for the furniture on layer two rather than layer one, the same move the whole cast is getting: the terrain profile draws stratum zero at z 2 and stratum one at z 0, so z 0 here puts a table in the second layer of rock instead of in front of the foreground. It matters where terrain overlaps the furniture; inside the open room nothing draws over it either way.

Set on every table and stool explicitly rather than left to the default, so a reader can see it was decided. The cast's own draw order is moving under the same directive and is owned by the agents holding the encounters those characters are introduced in - when that lands, re-check that the furniture still sits BEHIND the people standing at it."
editor_description = "THERE IS A SEAT FOR EVERYONE. Three tables and six stools, which covers the five who are standing - the Treasure Hunter, Rotini, the Miner, Quibble and the Keeper - with a pair spare for the rats, and Cheese Girl is serving rather than sitting. A cafe the whole cast has walked to and which seats two of them is a joke the shot did not mean to make.

They are set BETWEEN the cast rather than beside them, which is what a cafe forecourt looks like: people standing among their own furniture. The stools overlap the standing bodies at a few landing columns and that is fine - PropMarkers draws behind the cast, so a stool passes behind a rat instead of through him.

This one is the shop's own table, between the Treasure Hunter and Rotini.

Y IS -23, MEASURED. The level tunnel lays up to three cells of loose rock on the floor row, so furniture authored on the dig line is buried to the tabletop. local_tests/capture_cafe_gathering_stage.gd samples the same floor the controller uses at every column this furniture can slide to and prints the number. It moves a few pixels either way across the landing band, which is the accepted cost of props not being sampled."
position = Vector2(-346, -23)

